{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And his heart went faint</b> — his heart changed and departed from believing; his heart was not turning toward the matter. This is the language of the Mishnah: <b>מְפִיגִין טַעְמָן</b> (they dull their taste) (b. Beitza 14a), and similarly \"from lack of הֲפוּגוֹת\" (Lamentations 3:49), and \"וְרֵיחוֹ לֹא נָמָר\" (Jeremiah 48:11), which is translated \"וְרֵיחֵהּ לָא פָג\" — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 45:26). But it is not correct, for the meaning of the root פוג is stillness and cessation, as in \"Do not let yourself have פוּגַת\" (Lamentations 2:18), and likewise \"My eye flows and does not cease, from lack of הֲפֻגוֹת\" (Lamentations 3:49) — it flows constantly without stillness or interruption; and so <b>מְפִיגִין טַעְמָן</b> — they scatter it and nullify it; and so \"Therefore תָּפוּג Torah\" (Habakkuk 1:4) — it is annulled and cut off. And likewise here, \"and his heart went faint\" — his heart ceased and his breathing stopped, for the motion of the heart ceased and he was like a dead man. This phenomenon is well known when joy comes suddenly, and it is mentioned in the books of medicine, for the elderly and the weak cannot bear this — many among them faint when joy comes upon them suddenly and unexpectedly, for the heart suddenly expands and opens, and the innate heat goes out and scatters to the outer parts of the body, and the heart fails as it cools, and so the old man falls as though dead. And Scripture says, \"for he did not believe them,\" to inform us that a long time passed during that day while he lay motionless because he did not believe them — for it is well known in this type of fainting that they would call out to him and accustom him gradually to that joy until it settled in him peacefully. And this is the meaning of \"And they spoke to him all the words of Joseph which he had spoken to them, and he saw the wagons\" — they were calling the words of Joseph into his ears and bringing the wagons before him; then his spirit returned to him and his breathing resumed and he lived — and this is \"and the spirit of Jacob their father revived.\" And Onkelos translated \"and the spirit of prophecy rested,\" because the matter is true, he added this and interpreted it homiletically from the word רוּחַ, since it did not say \"and Jacob their father lived,\" but he made it like the matter of \"The רוּחַ of Adonai YHWH is upon me\" (Isaiah 61:1), \"And now Adonai YHWH has sent me and His רוּחַ\" (Isaiah 48:16), \"A man in whom is רוּחַ\" (Numbers 27:18)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:45:17.511356196Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}